@module flutter-m09 | Local Storage
@summary Persist data on the device with preferences, secure storage, SQLite, Drift and files.

@lesson flutter-m09-l01 | SharedPreferences | 8
## Learning objectives
- Store small key-value settings

## SharedPreferences
`shared_preferences` stores simple values (bool, int, double, String, List<String>) for settings and flags. Not for large or sensitive data.

```dart
import 'package:shared_preferences/shared_preferences.dart';

Future<void> saveTheme(bool dark) async {
  final prefs = SharedPreferencesAsync();
  await prefs.setBool('darkMode', dark);
}
```

## Summary
- Use it for small preferences only.

@lesson flutter-m09-l02 | Secure storage | 8
## Learning objectives
- Store secrets safely

## Secure storage
`flutter_secure_storage` uses the Android Keystore and iOS Keychain. Store tokens and keys there, never in SharedPreferences.

```dart
import 'package:flutter_secure_storage/flutter_secure_storage.dart';

const storage = FlutterSecureStorage();
Future<void> saveToken(String t) => storage.write(key: 'token', value: t);
```

## Summary
- Secrets belong in secure storage.

@lesson flutter-m09-l03 | SQLite | 8
## Learning objectives
- Understand relational local storage

## SQLite
SQLite is an embedded relational database: tables, rows, SQL queries, indexes and transactions. Use it for structured data that you query and filter, such as notes or lesson progress.

```dart
const createNotes = '''
CREATE TABLE notes(
  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
  title TEXT NOT NULL,
  created_at INTEGER NOT NULL
)''';
void main() => print(createNotes);
```

## Summary
- Use SQLite for structured, queryable data.

@lesson flutter-m09-l04 | sqflite | 8
## Learning objectives
- Use SQLite from Flutter

## sqflite
`sqflite` opens a database file and runs SQL. Keep access in a repository class.

```dart
import 'package:path/path.dart';
import 'package:sqflite/sqflite.dart';

Future<Database> open() async => openDatabase(
      join(await getDatabasesPath(), 'notes.db'),
      version: 1,
      onCreate: (db, v) => db.execute('CREATE TABLE notes(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, title TEXT)'),
    );
```

## Summary
- Use parameterised queries (whereArgs) to avoid SQL injection.

@lesson flutter-m09-l05 | Drift | 8
## Learning objectives
- Use a type-safe database layer

## Drift
Drift (formerly Moor) generates type-safe Dart APIs from table definitions, supports reactive queries (streams) and migrations. Run build_runner to generate code.

```dart
// Table definition (generated code via build_runner)
// class Notes extends Table {
//   IntColumn get id => integer().autoIncrement()();
//   TextColumn get title => text()();
// }
void main() {}
```

## Summary
- Drift gives typed queries and streams.

@lesson flutter-m09-l06 | Local caching | 8
## Learning objectives
- Serve data instantly and refresh in the background

## Local caching
Cache API responses locally so screens load instantly and work offline; then refresh from the network and update the cache (stale-while-revalidate).

```dart
Future<List<String>> lessons(Future<List<String>> Function() network, List<String>? cache, void Function(List<String>) save) async {
  try {
    final fresh = await network();
    save(fresh);
    return fresh;
  } catch (_) {
    return cache ?? [];
  }
}
```

## Summary
- Show cached data first, then refresh.

@lesson flutter-m09-l07 | File storage | 8
## Learning objectives
- Read and write files

## File storage
`path_provider` gives app directories; `dart:io` File reads and writes them. Use the documents directory for user data and the temporary directory for caches.

```dart
import 'dart:io';
import 'package:path_provider/path_provider.dart';

Future<File> saveText(String name, String text) async {
  final dir = await getApplicationDocumentsDirectory();
  return File('${dir.path}/$name').writeAsString(text);
}
```

## Summary
- Pick the right directory for each kind of file.

@lesson flutter-m09-l08 | Offline-first architecture | 8
## Learning objectives
- Make the local database the source of truth

## Offline-first architecture
In offline-first apps the UI reads from local storage only; a sync layer pulls from and pushes to the server when online, queuing changes made offline.

```dart
enum SyncStatus { synced, pending }

class LocalNote {
  LocalNote(this.id, this.title, this.status);
  final String id;
  final String title;
  final SyncStatus status;
}
```

## Summary
- UI reads local data; sync runs in the background.

@lesson flutter-m09-l09 | Database migrations | 8
## Learning objectives
- Change schemas without losing data

## Database migrations
When you add columns or tables, bump the database version and migrate in `onUpgrade`. Never drop users' data.

```dart
import 'package:sqflite/sqflite.dart';

Future<void> onUpgrade(Database db, int oldV, int newV) async {
  if (oldV < 2) await db.execute('ALTER TABLE notes ADD COLUMN pinned INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0');
}
```

## Summary
- Migrate step by step for each version.
